  "specimen_id": "bsd-tcp-4.2-1983",
  "canonical_name": "4.2BSD TCP (1983) -- the pre-congestion-control TCP that collapsed the Internet in 1986",
  "aliases": [
    "4.2BSD netinet/tcp",
    "TCP before slow start"
  ],
  "lineage": "TCP congestion-control lineage, the KNOWN-BAD ancestor: 4.2BSD's TCP (Bill Joy, Sam Leffler) had retransmission timers and windows but NO congestion window, NO slow start and a crude RTT estimate. In October 1986 the LBL-to-Berkeley path fell from 32 kbit/s to 40 bit/s -- congestion collapse -- which is the pressure that produced 4.3BSD-Tahoe's algorithms (Jacobson 1988). Preserved from the unix-history-repo (Spinellis) at tag BSD-4_2.",
  "domain": [
    "networks",
    "congestion-control",
    "retransmission",
    "contention",
    "failure"
  ],
